view":{"overview":"The Seahawks are 5-4 through week 9 of the 2026 season and have outscored opponents 202 to 162. The defense has done most of the work, ranking 5th in EPA allowed while the offense sits 23rd in EPA per play. Their most distinctive trait is the 3rd highest screen rate in the league.","offense":"The Seahawks offense has been well below average by EPA per play so far, and it has been much better passing the ball than running it. They have been one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the 3rd highest screen rate in the league. Sam Darnold has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Jaxon Smith-Njigba, who has drawn 36% of the targets.","defense":"The Seahawks have one of the best defenses in the league this season, ranking 5th or better in four of the five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area has been explosive play rate allowed, where it ranks 2nd, and it has no real weak spot. Byron Murphy has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Ernest Jones, who has 3 sacks, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is an eight man box rate that ranks 6th lowest in the league.","special":"The Seahawks have been one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 19% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers have made 89% of field goals, which is a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 23% of the money on the books.","players":"Sam Darnold has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,478 yards. Jaxon Smith-Njigba has been the clear top target with 36% of the looks and 1,036 receiving yards. The backfield has been a committee led by Emanuel Wilson and Jadarian Price. On defense, Byron Murphy leads the team with 4 sacks and Julian Love has picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Brian Fleury is calling the offense and Mike Macdonald is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2023 Broncos under Sean Payton and the 2023 Titans. Their defense looks most like the 2023 49ers and the 2023 Bears.","trends":"The offense went from 13th in EPA per play in 2025 to 23rd so far this season, a big step back. The defense moved from 2nd in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 5th so far this season, a modest decline. The biggest change in approach was screen rate, which went from 6% to 12%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}